#lang saffron
;; saffron-lang-plan S6 (G7): a `#map{...}` literal in a Saffron file has `any`
;; VALUES, so they need not agree.
;;
;; Same shape as the vector literal: `hamt-of` routes every value through
;; `tur-map-homog__`, so `#map{:a 1 :b "two"}` was "function 'tur-map-homog__'
;; arg 2: expected tyvar, got cstr".  Each value is wrapped in `(:: v any)` at
;; the literal, before the macro sees it.
;;
;; The KEYS are left alone.  They are already normalized to one key type by the
;; lowering above the widen, and a heterogeneous key would need `Hash[any]` and
;; `MapKey[any]` instances that do not exist -- hashing and comparing across
;; types at run time is a feature, not a lowering.
;;
;; This landed AFTER the interpreter's Map value tag was fixed, and the order
;; was measured rather than assumed.  A `(Map K any)` under `--interpret` used to
;; report `int` for every value with no diagnostic, so widening the literal first
;; would have made every Saffron map a silent wrong answer on one back end --
;; with the literal looking like the culprit.  See
;; docs/archive/map-of-any-is-broken-on-both-back-ends.md.
;;
;; NO requires.leak-check, and the reason is worth stating rather than leaving
;; to a missing file: a map LITERAL leaks its intermediate maps even when the
;; result is freed, because `hamt-of` chains `map-assoc` and only the last of
;; the N `{void* hamt}` boxes reaches `map-free`.  Measured PRE-EXISTING -- a
;; plain Turmeric `#map{:a 1 :b 2 :c 3}` leaks 1080 bytes in 11 allocations with
;; no `any` anywhere.  Filed as map-literal-leaks-its-intermediate-maps.
;;
;; 7.1 rather than 7.0: a truncating path through the box would print 7.

(defn main [] : int
  (let [m #map{:a 1 :b "two" :c 7.1}]
    (println (type-of (map-get m :a)))
    (println (type-of (map-get m :b)))
    (println (type-of (map-get m :c)))
    (println (cast (map-get m :c) float))
    (map-free m))
  0)
